Thank you Zhian, I used this script with the function poppr.msn()

obj <- df2genind(dat2, type = "PA", ploidy = 2)
diss.dist(obj)
x <- diss.dist(obj)
set.seed(9005)
poppr.msn(obj, x, palette = rainbow, sublist = "All" ,blacklist = NULL, gscale=TRUE, glim = c(0, 0.8), gadj=15, wscale=TRUE, .............)

Can I assume that edge thickness and color of my MSN are proportional to Bruvo's distance as in the Figure 19 of the manual example, which also uses dominant markers?

Thanks again
David

